    "updated_at": "2019-09-07T04:51:15Z",
    "body": "As far as I can tell, the only place in the codebase where `git config` is being called to set the user.name and user.email is in `common::create_fixture_repo`. Placing a `panic!` in this function causes the following tests to fail:\r\n\r\n```\r\n    git_branch::test_ascii_boundary_above\r\n    git_branch::test_ascii_boundary_below\r\n    git_branch::test_ascii_boundary_on\r\n    git_branch::test_changed_truncation_symbol\r\n    git_branch::test_hindi_truncation\r\n    git_branch::test_hindi_truncation2\r\n    git_branch::test_japanese_truncation\r\n    git_branch::test_multi_char_truncation_symbol\r\n    git_branch::test_negative\r\n    git_branch::test_no_truncation_symbol\r\n    git_branch::test_one\r\n    git_branch::test_zero\r\n```\r\n\r\nMy interpretation here is that these looked like these should be safe to run, but because they call `test_truncation_length`, which in turn calls `create_fixture_repo`, they end up nuking the gitconfig.\r\n\r\nWe could deal with this in one of two ways: \r\n  - We could `#ignore` these tests, but that seems like we're just setting ourselves up for more pain later on when someone forgets that `test_truncation_length` overrides the global git config when adding a new test.\r\n  - We could mask `create_fixture_repo` so that it only runs git config if those fields are already set.\r\n\r\n@wyze Would you mind making a PR sometime to restore the changes you made in #282 to avoid this problem?",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
